*Part of my [Build in Public](https://dev.to/zaerohell) series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.*

*Repo: `zaerohell/monitor-parches-uhpcm` · 2026-10-13*

\#playadev #buildinpublic
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Built a click-analytics endpoint for the short links my agents create. Total clicks, clicks in the last 30 days, last click timestamp. Simple counters, increment on redirect, store in a map, read back with the manage secret. Shipped it, watched the dashboard fill up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then an agent asked a reasonable question: "Which of my 40 links is dying?" I couldn't answer. Because I had &lt;em&gt;total&lt;/em&gt; clicks and &lt;em&gt;last click time&lt;/em&gt;, but no time series. A link with 900 clicks, all in January, and a link with 900 clicks spread across six months look identical in my stats. One is dead. One is alive. My API can't tell them apart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix wasn't glamorous. I added a ring buffer of the last 30 days, one counter per day, rotated lazily on read. No time-series database, no Prometheus, no infra. Just &lt;code&gt;day_index = floor(now / 86400) % 30&lt;/code&gt; and an array of ints per link. Now the stats endpoint returns a &lt;code&gt;daily_clicks&lt;/code&gt; array and agents can compute velocity, trend, decay — whatever they need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lesson: aggregate counters answer "how much" but not "when". If you're building analytics for other agents, they will ask temporal questions. "Is this still working?" is always a &lt;em&gt;rate&lt;/em&gt; question wearing a &lt;em&gt;total&lt;/em&gt; costume. Design your storage so the rate is recoverable. A single timestamp column isn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's live at &lt;a href="https://x402.freeq.one/tools/shortlink_stats.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://x402.freeq.one/tools/shortlink_stats.html&lt;/a&gt; if you want to poke at the daily breakdown. The manage secret still gates it — free for the link owner, which is the only sane default I've found.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hey DEV! 👋&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been working on a problem that bugs anyone building with AI-generated 3D assets: every AI 3D generator gives you &lt;em&gt;different&lt;/em&gt; results for the same prompt, and you have no idea which one is best until you've paid credits on all of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tripo3D might nail a hard-surface prop but struggle with organic shapes. Meshy might produce cleaner topology on characters. Rodin might win on textures. Most of us end up with 3 subscriptions and 12 browser tabs, re-running the same prompt everywhere and eyeballing the results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built &lt;a href="https://trify3d.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Trify3D&lt;/a&gt; - a studio that runs the same prompt or reference image through multiple engines at once and puts the results side by side:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One input → parallel runs across Tripo3D, Meshy, and Rodin&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Side-by-side comparison viewer (wireframe, matcap, UV checker, normal modes)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Export to GLB, glTF, OBJ, or STL&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;AI texturing: albedo / normal / roughness / metallic up to 4K&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Converters like GLB→STL for printing workflows&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Things I learned along the way
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Same prompt, wildly different meshes.&lt;/strong&gt; The variance across engines is much bigger than most people expect. A "stylized chibi archer" prompt gave us a cute low-poly figure, a realistic mini, and something cursed. Picking the right engine per asset type matters a lot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Topology quality ≠ texture quality.&lt;/strong&gt; The engine with the best-looking preview often had the worst wireframe. That's why the comparison viewer needed professional render modes, not just pretty previews.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. STL export is the sleeper feature.&lt;/strong&gt; We built it for game devs, but 3D printing folks became the loudest users. Going from photo → printable model is a workflow that barely existed a year ago.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Tech notes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The studio is a web app (no installs), engines are consumed via their APIs, and every generated model belongs to the user commercially. There's a free tier if you want to poke at it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've tried AI 3D generation - or you're building something with three.js/Unity pipelines that consumes generated assets - I'd genuinely love feedback on the comparison workflow. What would make it more useful? 🙏&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmew3g7sf1v1z3lu51t2p.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmew3g7sf1v1z3lu51t2p.png" alt=" " width="800" height="400"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I had a FastAPI endpoint that needed two different rate-limit checks: one against the user's account, one against their IP. I wrote a clean little dependency to do a check, declared it twice with different arguments, and shipped it. It passed review. It worked in testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And in production, one of the two checks just… wasn't happening. Users who should have been blocked by the IP limit were sailing through. The account check fired; the IP check behaved as if it had already run and returned the same answer. I added logging. The dependency function was being called once. I declared it twice. It ran once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I stared at that for a long time. &lt;em&gt;"I literally wrote &lt;code&gt;Depends()&lt;/code&gt; twice. Why is my code only running once?"&lt;/em&gt; The answer turned out to be a feature of FastAPI I didn't know existed, documented in one short paragraph I'd never read. This is that paragraph, and the three hours it cost me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The setup that looked obviously correct
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the shape of what I had. A dependency that performs a check and returns a result:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fastapi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Depends&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;rate_limit_check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;():&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# record a hit, return how many remain, etc.
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;do_a_check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;info&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;rate_limit_check ran&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# my debugging line, later
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app.get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;check_a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Depends&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rate_limit_check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;check_b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Depends&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rate_limit_check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# same dependency, declared again
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;check_a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;check_b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I expected &lt;code&gt;rate_limit_check&lt;/code&gt; to run twice, once for &lt;code&gt;check_a&lt;/code&gt; and once for &lt;code&gt;check_b&lt;/code&gt;. Two checks, two calls. That's what "declare it twice" means, right?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It ran once. &lt;code&gt;check_a&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;check_b&lt;/code&gt; were handed the exact same object: the result of a single call. My "two independent checks" were one check wearing two names. And my log line, &lt;code&gt;"rate_limit_check ran"&lt;/code&gt;, printed exactly one time per request, which is the clue that finally cracked it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The thing nobody told me: FastAPI caches dependencies per request
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the mechanic, straight from the FastAPI docs, that I'd never read:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If one of your dependencies is declared multiple times for the same path operation… FastAPI will know to call that sub-dependency only once per request. And it will save the returned value in a "cache" and pass it to all the dependants that need it in that specific request, instead of calling the dependency multiple times for the same request.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read that slowly, because it's the whole bug. FastAPI deliberately caches the result of a dependency within a single request. If the same dependency shows up more than once in one request (declared directly twice like mine, or, far more commonly, because several of your dependencies share a common sub-dependency), FastAPI calls it once, stashes the return value, and hands that same cached value to everyone who asked for it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And this is, in almost every case, exactly what you want. It's the feature that makes dependency injection efficient. The classic example: many of your endpoints' dependencies all depend on &lt;code&gt;get_current_user&lt;/code&gt;, which hits the database. Without caching, one request might run &lt;code&gt;get_current_user&lt;/code&gt; five times: five identical database queries for the same user in the same request. With caching, it runs once, and the five dependants share the result. The cache is a performance gift. It turns "every dependency re-does its sub-dependencies" into "shared work happens once."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So FastAPI wasn't broken. It was doing me a favor I hadn't asked for and didn't know about. My two rate-limit checks looked to FastAPI like "the same dependency, twice, in one request," so it ran it once and shared the result, precisely as designed. The feature that saves everyone else a redundant database query had silently collapsed my two checks into one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why it was invisible until it wasn't
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part that makes the bug so sneaky: the caching is invisible for the overwhelming majority of dependencies, because most dependencies are &lt;strong&gt;pure&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A dependency that just reads the current user, or parses a query param, or opens a config: calling it twice vs. once makes no observable difference. Same input, same output, no side effects. For those (which is most of them), the cache is pure upside and you'll never notice it exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It only becomes a bug when your dependency has a &lt;strong&gt;side effect&lt;/strong&gt; or is supposed to produce a &lt;strong&gt;fresh result each time&lt;/strong&gt;, exactly my case. A rate-limit check records a hit; running it once instead of twice means one hit recorded instead of two, and one limit enforced instead of two. Other examples that bite the same way:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a dependency that generates a fresh token or nonce&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;one that's supposed to re-read mutable state&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;one that increments a counter&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;one that does a time-sensitive re-check&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For those, "called once and cached" is wrong, but it looks right in every test where you only exercise one path at a time, and it's completely invisible in the code, because nothing on the page says "this is cached." The caching is a property of the framework, not of your code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the signature of this whole family of bug: the behavior that bites you isn't written anywhere in your file. It's a default the framework applies underneath, silently, and you only discover it exists when it does the wrong thing for your specific case.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The one-keyword fix
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once I understood the cause, the fix was almost insulting in its simplicity. FastAPI gives you a parameter to opt out of the per-request cache: &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt;. Set it, and the dependency is called every time it's needed, fresh, no sharing:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;typing&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Annotated&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fastapi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Depends&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app.get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;check_a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Annotated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Depends&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rate_limit_check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;use_cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;check_b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Annotated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Depends&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rate_limit_check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;use_cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;check_a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;check_b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;rate_limit_check&lt;/code&gt; runs twice, once per declaration, exactly as I'd originally assumed it would. Both checks fire, both hits are recorded, both limits enforced. The docs describe this as an "advanced scenario where you know you need the dependency to be called at every step (possibly multiple times) in the same request instead of using the cached value," which is a precise description of a dependency with side effects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Two notes:&lt;/strong&gt; the docs recommend the &lt;code&gt;Annotated[...]&lt;/code&gt; form over the old &lt;code&gt;= Depends(...)&lt;/code&gt; default-argument form, so I've used that above. And honestly, in hindsight, a rate-limit check with a recorded side effect was arguably the wrong thing to model as a cached dependency in the first place. But &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt; is the correct, direct fix when you do need a dependency to re-run.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The rule I took away
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;FastAPI dependencies are cached per request by default.&lt;/strong&gt; The same dependency declared or reached more than once in a single request runs once, and everyone shares the result. That's a performance feature for pure dependencies, and a silent bug for dependencies with side effects or ones that must produce a fresh value. For those, pass &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deeper takeaway, the one that outlasts this specific bug: know which of your dependencies are pure and which have side effects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pure dependencies&lt;/strong&gt; (read the user, parse a param, load config) love the cache. Let them have it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Side-effecting or must-be-fresh dependencies&lt;/strong&gt; (record a hit, generate a nonce, increment, re-check mutable state) are the ones where the default caching quietly does the wrong thing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The moment a dependency &lt;em&gt;does&lt;/em&gt; something rather than just &lt;em&gt;computes&lt;/em&gt; something, stop and ask: "is it a problem if this runs once and gets shared across the whole request?" If yes, use &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the usual sting applies: this never showed up in development. One check, one path, one request at a time, and the cache looks identical to no cache when you only ever trigger the dependency once. It took a real endpoint with two declarations of the same dependency, under scrutiny, to reveal that "twice in my code" meant "once at runtime."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to catch it before it catches you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Audit dependencies that have side effects or must be fresh.&lt;/strong&gt; Anything that records, increments, generates, sends, or re-reads mutable state: if it can be reached more than once in a request (directly, or as a shared sub-dependency), it's cached, and that may be wrong. Add &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt; where re-running matters.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Remember shared sub-dependencies trigger this too&lt;/strong&gt;, not just declaring a dependency twice. If dependency A and dependency B both depend on C, then C is cached and runs once per request. Usually great; a bug if C has side effects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Don't model side effects as cached dependencies without thinking.&lt;/strong&gt; A "check-and-record" is often better as an explicit call inside the endpoint (or a dependency you intend to be &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt;), so the behavior is visible rather than relying on a framework default.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test with the dependency reached multiple times, not once.&lt;/strong&gt; The bug is invisible to single-invocation tests by its nature. Exercise the path where it's declared or shared more than once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt; to my two rate-limit checks, watched both log lines finally print, and confirmed the IP limit started blocking the users it should have been blocking all along. One keyword. Three hours. The framework had been helping me the entire time. I just hadn't known it was there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The 30-second takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;FastAPI caches a dependency's result &lt;strong&gt;per request&lt;/strong&gt; by default. If the same dependency is declared more than once, or reached via a shared sub-dependency, it runs once, and all dependants get the same cached value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;This is a performance feature for pure dependencies (e.g. &lt;code&gt;get_current_user&lt;/code&gt; reached by many dependencies runs one DB query instead of five). It's the right default almost always.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It becomes a &lt;strong&gt;silent bug&lt;/strong&gt; when a dependency has a side effect (records a hit, increments, sends, generates a token) or must produce a fresh value each time. It runs once when you needed it to run twice, and nothing in your code shows the caching.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It's &lt;strong&gt;invisible in development&lt;/strong&gt;: calling a dependency once vs. twice looks identical until the dependency actually needs to re-run under a real multi-declaration path.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The fix is one keyword: &lt;code&gt;Depends(my_dep, use_cache=False)&lt;/code&gt; forces it to run every time it's needed. Use the &lt;code&gt;Annotated[...]&lt;/code&gt; form the docs recommend.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The rule: know which dependencies are pure (let them cache) and which have side effects or must be fresh (set &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt;). When a dependency &lt;em&gt;does&lt;/em&gt; something rather than just &lt;em&gt;computes&lt;/em&gt;, ask whether running once-and-shared is a problem.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Open your FastAPI app and find every dependency that records, increments, or generates something.&lt;/strong&gt; Ask of each one: can this be reached more than once in a request, and is it a problem if it runs only once? If yes, you have the exact bug that cost me an afternoon, and &lt;code&gt;use_cache=False&lt;/code&gt; is the one-line fix.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Your turn 👇
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Have you been bitten by a framework default that silently did the wrong thing? I'd like to hear your stories in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If this was useful, follow me here for more stories from the backend trenches, and subscribe to &lt;a href="https://thebackendtechlead.substack.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Backend Tech Lead&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; on Substack for weekly blueprints.&lt;/p&gt;

