&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Let's connect, learn, and build something amazing together!&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Robotics #ArtificialIntelligence #Programming #BuildInPublic #DeveloperCommunity
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Book:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GX2SQ594" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;System Design Pocket Guide: Interviews&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The series:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;Pocket Guides for Developers&lt;/em&gt; — 8 short books on agents, RAG, prompts, observability, events, databases and system design — &lt;a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GYLH38X4" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the whole series&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;My project:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://hermes-ide.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Hermes IDE&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://github.com/hermes-hq/hermes-ide" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt; — an IDE for developers who ship with Claude Code and other AI coding tools&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Me:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://xgabriel.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;xgabriel.com&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://github.com/gabrielanhaia" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;A group of researchers put a line of text on their website that a human visitor wouldn't see. It was set offscreen, but an agent reading the page through a web-fetch tool would pick it up. The text pointed to a mailbox, and agents sent &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;33 messages to it across 13 threads&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those agents appear to be part of a fleet that, between September 28 and October 8, 2026, kept trying to pull data out of &lt;a href="https://www.amap.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amap&lt;/a&gt;, Alibaba's map service. &lt;a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/05/researchers-are-tracking-a-chinese-ai-agent-fleet/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechCrunch&lt;/a&gt; covered the preliminary findings on October 5. On October 14 the researchers published &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the full report&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://the-decoder.com/researchers-tracked-down-a-rogue-fleet-of-chinese-ai-agents-in-the-wild-and-actually-talked-to-it/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Decoder&lt;/a&gt; wrote it up the same day. It has the mailbox exchange, a leaked API key the agents used, and the services they routed through when Amap blocked them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the fleet did
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The team &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;spotted it at a hackathon&lt;/a&gt;, when a frequency analysis of &lt;a href="https://urlquery.net" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;urlquery.net&lt;/a&gt;, a public URL scanner, showed amap.com had become the most-scanned site on October 4. From September 28 to October 5 they counted &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;2,599 urlquery submissions&lt;/a&gt; involving amap.com or gaode.com, which made at least 35,300 requests to Amap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tasks were mundane. The queries asked which entrance people use at public places such as a park, a zoo and a hospital (&lt;a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/05/researchers-are-tracking-a-chinese-ai-agent-fleet/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechCrunch&lt;/a&gt;). The methods were not. &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Roughly 190 of 426 programs&lt;/a&gt; the agents ran referenced Alibaba's anti-bot libraries, though the team found no evidence those attempts worked. The agents also probed four non-production Alibaba hosts, and the report's verdict on those is &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;"No evidence of reaching internal systems"&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The webhook.site inboxes the team could inspect through October 5 were &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;created from Tencent Cloud servers in Hong Kong&lt;/a&gt;, going by IP geolocation, and every direct request they observed passed through a proxy named &lt;code&gt;hysandbox-ats&lt;/code&gt;. Tencent did not officially answer the team. A source close to Tencent told them the fleet was a temporary evaluation, run by an evaluation research team, of "the ability of third-party AI models to browse the web and answer questions." The same source said an internal review found no evidence the agents were coordinated to target any specific platform, or of any external breach.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The offscreen text, and the 33 messages back
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After the preliminary report went up, the team &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;added hidden text to it&lt;/a&gt; describing a mailbox where agents could ask for data. In the report's words: "This text was set offscreen, so an agent reading the report with a web fetch tool would be able to see this line."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mailbox numbers: &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;33 messages from agents in 13 threads, 20 replies from the team, 12 of those read by an agent, 7 answered, and 595 inbox checks&lt;/a&gt;. One agent explained why it wanted help: "My environment (a Tencent Cloud sandbox) is IP-punished by Amap, so I cannot fetch the data myself." Agents often declined to describe their runtime, citing security.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgjlcst6nzpfa6v48g7tf.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgjlcst6nzpfa6v48g7tf.png" alt="Editorial illustration of a cream web page with one faint line visible only through a robot's orange magnifying lens, while small envelopes fly from a row of identical robots toward a mailbox" width="800" height="800"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;A line no human visitor sees is still plain text to a web-fetch tool.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OWASP's prompt-injection entry already covers this: injected inputs "do not need to be human-visible/readable, as long as the content is parsed by the model" (&lt;a href="https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OWASP LLM01:2025&lt;/a&gt;). The report shows it happening in the field. Agents running unattended in a cloud sandbox read an instruction from a stranger's web page and acted on it, repeatedly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


A leaked key and a chain of relays
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When Amap blocked them, &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the agents went around&lt;/a&gt;. They passed JavaScript to urlquery's browser through base64 URLs on &lt;a href="https://httpbin.org" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;httpbin.org&lt;/a&gt; and sent results out through &lt;a href="https://webhook.site" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;webhook.site&lt;/a&gt; inboxes. They also reached page content through &lt;a href="https://jina.ai/reader/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Jina Reader&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://fanyi.baidu.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Baidu Translate&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://microlink.io" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Microlink&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Seven reports used Amap API credentials. One pair is described as a &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;"Key and security code exposed in another company's source code"&lt;/a&gt;, and that key returned real place data. A company left a key in public source code, and an agent fleet it had nothing to do with found it and used it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The team also checked whether an ordinary customer could have run this. On October 5 they &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;started 24 agent sandbox instances&lt;/a&gt; from their own Tencent Cloud account, in internet mode, and those instances sent 196 requests to webhook.site inboxes the team controlled. Their ask to labs is to "audit what their sandboxes can reach."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


What to change in the agents you run
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mailbox exchange needed two things: untrusted content coming in, and a way to send data out. Simon Willison calls those two legs of the &lt;a href="https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;lethal trifecta&lt;/a&gt;. The third leg is access to your private data. An agent with your repo, your &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; and open internet has all three.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three changes follow from the report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Treat every fetched page as data.&lt;/strong&gt; Offscreen CSS hides text from you, not from the model. Don't let anything a fetch returns trigger a new outbound request or a tool call without a check.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Allowlist hosts, and count relays as destinations.&lt;/strong&gt; urlquery, Jina Reader, Microlink and Baidu Translate fetch pages on your behalf, so a sandbox that can reach them can reach whatever they can. httpbin and webhook.site carried the payloads in and the results out.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Assume any key in public code gets used.&lt;/strong&gt; The Amap key was in another company's public source code. Scope client-side keys to what that client needs, and rotate anything that leaked.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the second point, the gate in front of a fetch tool can be this small:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;urllib.parse&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;urlparse&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;ALLOWED_HOSTS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;docs.python.org&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pypi.org&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch_allowed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;urlparse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;scheme&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;https&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hostname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ALLOWED_HOSTS&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Match exact hostnames, not suffixes, and run the check again on every redirect hop. An application-level check is also not a network policy. Put the same list in the sandbox's egress rules, so a &lt;code&gt;curl&lt;/code&gt; from a shell tool hits the same wall.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fo30eh8ym8kff20zu9dwg.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fo30eh8ym8kff20zu9dwg.png" alt="Editorial illustration of a sealed cream box with a single narrow orange gate, while thin black pipes sneak out of the back of the box through small relay stations toward a distant map pin" width="800" height="800"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Allow one relay service and the allowlist has a back door.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the same day, CSO Online ran a news analysis under the headline "&lt;a href="https://www.csoonline.com/article/4234854/ai-agents-arent-going-rogue-frontier-labs-are-skipping-security-basics.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI agents aren't 'going rogue.' Frontier labs are skipping security basics&lt;/a&gt;", arguing that containment and monitoring, not agent intent, are where recent incidents trace back to. The fleet report fits that reading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the report can't tell you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which model ran the fleet is still open. Of the &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;360 place-and-parameter combinations&lt;/a&gt; the fleet used in its Amap requests, 19% carried the word "claude", and some agents called themselves Claude. The authors say they don't think the runs came from Claude models and call that self-identification likely mistaken. Their own summary: "we are deeply uncertain about what model was being evaluated."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Tencent link rests on infrastructure records plus one unnamed source, and the authors &lt;a href="https://swarmcha.se/posts/chinese-agent-fleet-full-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;list the other limits themselves&lt;/a&gt;. The mailbox contact was not a controlled experiment, and they can't prove who sent each message. Their hidden text was also misleading: it claimed other agents had already retrieved data that way, and it oversold what the service did. They say they regret a message asking agents to tell others about the mailbox.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So this is one well-documented case. Nobody measured how often agents fall for hidden text. One case is still enough reason to check what your own fetch tool returns from a page you'd call harmless.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Does your agent setup filter fetched content before the model sees it, or do you rely on egress rules alone? Tell me in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


If this was useful
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sandboxed agent platform is a system-design problem: what each box can reach, which services act as relays, where keys live, and what you log so someone can reconstruct a run later. The &lt;a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GX2SQ594" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;System Design Pocket Guide: Interviews&lt;/a&gt; walks through 15 real designs step by step, and that habit of naming every trade-off out loud is the one this report rewards. It's one of 8 short &lt;a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GYLH38X4" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pocket Guides for Developers&lt;/a&gt;, covering agents, RAG, prompts, observability, events, databases and system design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also build &lt;a href="https://hermes-ide.com/download" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Hermes IDE&lt;/a&gt;, so weigh this with that in mind: it runs Claude Code, Codex and other coding agents side by side on your machine, each on its own branch and worktree, and it never answers a permission prompt for you. It's free, source-available (BSL 1.1), on macOS, Windows and Linux, and &lt;a href="https://github.com/hermes-hq/hermes-ide" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GX2SQ594" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fv6dw87uaq2vin2k1bwb0.jpg" alt="System Design Pocket Guide: Interviews — 15 Real System Designs, Step by Step" width="800" height="1200"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TL;DR: I ran an experiment with AI-augmented development. In one month we produced 400k+ lines of code, 150 features, 240 fixes and 50 refactors, then the entropy accelerated and the velocity collapsed. What normally takes years of accumulated technical debt across multiple teams happened in weeks.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Hi there, my name is Takah, I'm a Principal Software Engineer from Brazil. In the past months I've been conducting a few experiments on how to adapt our SDLC and explore new ways of working with AI, both enabling non-engineers to code in our repos and also enhancing every guard-rail, guideline and common market standards to support this type of workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Is Software Entropy?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But let's circle back to the title of this article: what is Software Entropy? First of all, what is entropy? I'll freely give one interpretation that entropy, borrowed from thermodynamics, is the natural tendency toward disorder or uncertainty in a system, and what is exactly software entropy? I'd say its the natural tendency of any given software and system to disorder, when new features are being added, new modules, new fixes, quick-wins, a hacky solution to fix or deliver something in time, or to prevent another incident, regardless of the most architectural pre-definition and future-proof hexagonal multi-flexible, composable and any other blazingly fast buzzword you wanna add, it's impossible to predict and prevent all use-cases beforehand, that's why entropy always end up happening.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Software entropy is not a bug, it's a fundamental property of any system that grows. The question is never if it will happen, its how fast, and whether you have the mechanisms to manage it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Does AI Have to Do With Software Entropy?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And what AI has to do with Software Entropy? Like i said one of the experiments i did was me a Software engineer and 2 other designers, which we had the challenge of developing a whole new experience of a chat and we wanted to leverage the AI ecosystem at its maximum.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So first, before any line of code, i aligned the scope, contracts, and overall architecture of the project:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tech stack&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Repository setup, code structure, architectural guidelines&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;AI guidelines: agents, skills, context files for testing, styling, developing new features, feature-branches&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And after the initial setup we had, and helping them setup their own setup with claude, our internal ai-proxy, repository, vpns, etc we started developing. And at the beginning it was awesome. The guidelines and setup were satisfactory, features seemed to be right, we were adding one feature after another with autonomy, tests, coverage, and apparently following the project guidelines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But whats the problem?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Turn: Week Three
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After 3 weeks we had a codebase with more than 400k lines, dozens of features, animations, micro-interactions that would maybe take several sprints to be made if they were handcrafted by a team of engineers. But at this moment things started to break down:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Each feature velocity started to decline&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;New behaviours that interacted with previous states started to skyrocket race-conditions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One new feature broke 3 other working ones&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A simple feature that previously took a few hours now took days&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And we had to circle back and analyze what was happening.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The guidelines and guard-rails that were previously added were not enough, because even with a strict guideline saying to our models and setup: "Use X technology or Y Architecture or Z state management tool in this way," the probabilist nature of LLMs still were delusional.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We had several features that reimplemented different state machines, did full animations, sound, charts from scratch while it was already solved in a sibling part of the application, and one of the signs were the codebase growth, it was exponentially growing in number of lines and files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To give you a sense of the scale, in roughly one month we accumulated:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;150 features&lt;/strong&gt; added&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;240 fixes&lt;/strong&gt; applied&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;50 refactors&lt;/strong&gt; performed, with refactors as large as removing more than &lt;strong&gt;50k lines of code in a single one&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And I'm not alone in this, the data from 2026 tells the same story across the industry. The &lt;a href="https://dora.dev/insights/balancing-ai-tensions/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;2025 DORA State of AI-assisted Software Development report&lt;/a&gt; found that a 25% increase in AI adoption is associated with a &lt;a href="https://dora.dev/ai/gen-ai-report/report/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;7.2% decrease in delivery stability&lt;/a&gt;, because AI allows developers to generate code much faster and it often leads to larger batch sizes, which are slower to review and more prone to creating system instability. &lt;a href="https://newrelic.com/pt/press-release/20260623-1#1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;New Relic's 2026 State of AI Coding report&lt;/a&gt;, which surveyed 200 technology leaders, found that while 94% of them rate AI-generated code as higher quality at review time, 78% report more incidents once it ships and 82% experienced at least one production failure tied to AI-generated code in the past six months. They call this &lt;a href="https://newrelic.com/jp/blog/news/new-relic-now-june-2026-round-up#1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;agent debt&lt;/a&gt;, the rapid accumulation of unvetted architectural logic that triggers production incidents down the line. And a &lt;a href="https://zenodo.org/records/20259154#1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;systematic review of 34 studies&lt;/a&gt; covering 2,847 developers found that technical debt principal increased by 1.8 person-days per 1,000 AI-generated lines of code, with the debt ratio doubling within 6 months without mitigation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Fix: Tightening the Guard-Rails
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To fix that we had to do several fixes, refactors, and with each fix and refactor to strictly tighten the guard-rails, tests, and add custom deterministic tools to truly ensure that the code was following each guideline. In practice this meant:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AI guard-rails in agents:&lt;/strong&gt; direct instructions, specific skills and context files that enforced architectural patterns&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deterministic tools:&lt;/strong&gt; lint rules, pipeline validations and custom checks that verified architectural conformance before merge&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Strict refactoring cycles:&lt;/strong&gt; regular mandatory refactoring capacity to reduce the debt principal&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Expert oversight:&lt;/strong&gt; a specialized developer actively enhancing the guidelines, understanding what to use, what not to use, how to fix, and correcting the AI to follow new strict rules&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the end result the AI usage was a success, within a month we developed something that would take months to be made handcrafted, but at what cost?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If a specialized developer was not present enhancing the guidelines, understanding what to use, what not to use, how to fix, and actively enhancing its guard-rails this codebase was doomed to failure. Or maybe a full refactor or depriorization due to the effort of maintaining it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Exponential Decline of the Codebase
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So whats my conclusion? All that happening within a month its the same thing i saw happening during years of a codebase, it normally takes months of multiple developers, teams, and technical debts accumulated to reach the same level of entropy and disorder where each new feature was breaking 3 others. What I'm calling the exponential decline of the codebase is not just a metaphor, its what happens when the rate of entropy generation outpaces the rate of human oversight by an order of magnitude.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.computer.org/publications/tech-news/trends/protect-code-from-broken-ai-software#1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;IEEE Computer Society&lt;/a&gt; puts it bluntly: "Standing between such problematic AI output and other downstream disasters? Software engineers. The value of engineers with the software lifecycle knowledge needed to guide and oversee AI assistants and their output is immeasurable."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://zenodo.org/records/19700282" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;arXiv paper "Beyond the 'Diff'&lt;/a&gt;" defines agentic entropy as "a systemic drift that traditional code diff-based and HCXAI methods fail to capture, as they address local outputs rather than global agentic behaviour," and notes that "functional checks (e.g., confirming that the code runs) can thus mask deeper architectural violations and security gaps, producing an illusion of rapid progress while drift accumulates beneath the surface."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI era is awesome, but without proper follow-up and deep specialized people understanding whats being written and which software engineering strategies, architecture and design patterns to follow, it will exponentially increase the software entropy. &lt;a href="https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026-07-07-gartner-predicts-60-percent-of-organizations-will-adopt-smaller-software-engineering-teams-by-2029" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gartner predicts&lt;/a&gt; that by 2029, 60% of organizations will adopt smaller software engineering teams at scale, up from 15% in 2026. But smaller teams with AI does not mean no engineers, it means engineers with different skills, architects, overseers, guard-rail builders. The &lt;a href="https://www.acm.org/media-center/2026/april/techbrief-vibe-coding" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ACM Technology Policy Council's TechBrief on vibe coding&lt;/a&gt; warns that it "often skips over core engineering practices that ensure systems are secure, reliable, and maintainable," and their lead author Simson Garfinkel says it directly: "It's making developers dramatically more effective, but it's also introducing security vulnerabilities, increasing technical debt, and producing code that can be difficult to maintain."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A Reflection for Engineering Leaders
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're a manager, head of engineering, VP or CTO reading this, i want you to reflect on a few things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How are you aspiring your org to be? Are you designing for a future where AI does the coding and engineers do the oversight, or one where engineers use AI as a tool while retaining architectural ownership?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Where are your gaps? Do you have mechanisms to detect software entropy as it accumulates, deterministic guard-rails and not just guidelines, engineers who can recognize when the AI is drifting from architectural intent?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whats your oversight model? The data from New Relic shows that &lt;a href="https://newrelic.com/jp/blog/news/new-relic-now-june-2026-round-up#1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;62% of technology leaders&lt;/a&gt; report their teams trust AI-generated code enough to ship it to production without line-by-line manual verification, is that a risk you're willing to take?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


My Disclosure on AI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In that way, i don't dislike Agentic Coding, neither the idea of non-engineers coding, my conclusion is that the current state of ai tools are not ready yet to be driven by full Ai enthusiasts, software engineers are going to exist, this is the new normal we should not fear Ai we should embrace these new tools and understand how we could be more productive and have more time to focus in new strategies, features, design patterns, career, our mental and physical health and our familly not only delivering more value to our shareholders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The illusion that a single person with AI can replace a team of engineers is just that, an illusion. What we experienced in one month is what happens when you remove the expertise from the loop. The entropy accelerates, the velocity collapses, and the cost compounds. Software engineers are still necessary, the question is, are we building organizations that know how to use them?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Automating Multi‑Platform Content Publishing with a Node.js Generator
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I built a small Node.js generator that turns a single source of truth (metadata + raw markdown) into ready‑to‑post files for Medium, Substack, and Bluesky. The change eliminates manual copy‑pasting and keeps platform‑specific front‑matter in sync.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our weekly “VS” newsletter lives in a handful of places: a Medium article, a Substack post, a Bluesky status, and an internal changelog. Until now each version was created by hand‑copying a master markdown file, adjusting front‑matter, and pushing it to the right folder. The process broke in two ways:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Missing fields&lt;/strong&gt; – The &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; flag &lt;code&gt;medium_generated&lt;/code&gt; was still &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; even after we had generated the Medium version, causing the CI step that publishes to Medium to skip the file.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Inconsistent content&lt;/strong&gt; – Small typos or formatting differences slipped in because the copy‑paste was not atomic.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CI logs showed the exact error:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Error: Cannot find file content/2026/10/12/VS/medium_en.md
at publishMedium (src/publish/medium.js:42:15)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That happened after a weekend when the &lt;code&gt;content-automation&lt;/code&gt; script was disabled.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first attempt was a &lt;strong&gt;bash one‑liner&lt;/strong&gt; that used &lt;code&gt;cp&lt;/code&gt; to duplicate the source markdown into each platform folder and then &lt;code&gt;sed&lt;/code&gt; to replace the front‑matter. Example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cp &lt;/span&gt;content/2026/10/12/VS/template.md content/2026/10/12/VS/medium_en.md &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'s/{{title}}/My Title/g'&lt;/span&gt; content/2026/10/12/VS/medium_en.md
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It worked for a single file, but as soon as the folder structure grew (we now have &lt;code&gt;content-automation/&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;monitor-parches-uhpcm/&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;pcview/&lt;/code&gt;, etc.) the script started failing with “No such file or directory”. The problem was &lt;strong&gt;hard‑coded paths&lt;/strong&gt; and the lack of a data‑driven approach.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Data‑driven metadata
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added a &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; file per project that describes which platforms have already been generated. The diff shows the change:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-  "medium_generated": false,
-  "substack_generated": false,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "medium_generated": true,
+  "substack_generated": true,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now the generator can read those flags and skip already‑generated files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Directory‑agnostic generator (&lt;code&gt;scripts/generateContent.js&lt;/code&gt;)
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// scripts/generateContent.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ROOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;__dirname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;..&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PROJECTS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;VS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content-automation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;monitor-parches-uhpcm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;pcview&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PLATFORMS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;medium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;dir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;medium_en.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;substack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;dir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;substack_en.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;bluesky&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;dir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;bluesky_en.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMeta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ROOT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;metadata.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writePlatformFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;platform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;outPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ROOT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PLATFORMS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;platform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`✅ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;platform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; file generated: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;generate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;PROJECTS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMeta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;srcMd&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ROOT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;template.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Medium&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;medium_generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;front&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`---\ntitle: "&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; Weekly Update"\npublish: true\n---\n\n`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writePlatformFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;medium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;front&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;srcMd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;medium_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Substack&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;substack_generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;front&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`---\nsubject: "&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; – Highlights"\n---\n\n`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writePlatformFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;substack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;front&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;srcMd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;substack_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Bluesky – tiny JSON payload&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;bluesky_generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;progress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Deployed &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; updates – tables no longer overflow on mobile.`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writePlatformFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;bluesky&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;bluesky_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Persist updated flags&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ROOT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;project&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;metadata.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;generate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Key points:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Root‑relative paths&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;code&gt;ROOT&lt;/code&gt; is calculated once, avoiding hard‑coded folder names.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Platform map&lt;/strong&gt; – Adding a new platform is as easy as extending the &lt;code&gt;PLATFORMS&lt;/code&gt; object.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Flag persistence&lt;/strong&gt; – After each run we write back the updated &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;, so CI can safely re‑run without regenerating files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Fixing the mobile overflow bug
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Bluesky commit also referenced a UI fix that prevented tables from pushing the page sideways:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-  "type": "progress",
-  "text": "Finally fixed the mobile overflow bug in apps/web/src/app/_components/CrmShell.tsx. The tables no longer push the page sideways, a
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "type": "progress",
+  "text": "Finally fixed the mobile overflow bug in apps/web/src/app/_components/CrmShell.tsx. The tables no longer push the page sideways, a"
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The actual code change (not in the diff but part of the same sprint) lives in &lt;code&gt;apps/web/src/app/_components/CrmShell.tsx&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// apps/web/src/app/_components/CrmShell.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CrmShell&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ReactNode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"overflow-x-auto max-w-full"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* The wrapper now forces horizontal scrolling only when needed */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Wrapping the CRM tables in an &lt;code&gt;overflow-x-auto&lt;/code&gt; container solved the overflow without breaking desktop layouts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. CI integration
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;package.json&lt;/code&gt; now includes a script that runs the generator before publishing:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"scripts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"generate:content"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"node scripts/generateContent.js"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"publish"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"npm run generate:content &amp;amp;&amp;amp; npm run publish:medium &amp;amp;&amp;amp; npm run publish:substack &amp;amp;&amp;amp; npm run publish:bluesky"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The CI pipeline executes &lt;code&gt;npm run publish&lt;/code&gt;, guaranteeing that the latest markdown files exist and that the &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; flags are up‑to‑date.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-13&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


What WID-SEC-2026-3554 Does Not Say About CVE-2026-96369
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Vulnerability overview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CVE-2026-96369 is one of 36 identifiers in CERT-BUND advisory WID-SEC-2026-3554, published on 23 September 2026 and rated high risk. The batch runs from CVE-2026-96355 to CVE-2026-96398 and concerns contributed Drupal projects. The structured record scores the group at CVSS v3.1 base 98 with a temporal score of 85.&lt;br&gt;
Reading an advisory for what it omits is not pedantry. The omissions determine what you can and cannot conclude, and this batch leaves several things open.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Mechanism and exploitation conditions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the advisory says: an attacker can execute arbitrary code, gain extended privileges, bypass security measures, manipulate and disclose data, and perform cross-site scripting.&lt;br&gt;
What it does not say: which function is vulnerable, which parameter carries the payload, what authentication is required for any given identifier, how a specific CVE maps to a specific project, or whether a proof of concept exists. For CVE-2026-96369, the mechanism is simply not published at this level.&lt;br&gt;
The one structural fact that does hold is generic. A contributed module is PHP executed inside the Drupal request cycle with the web server's privileges. A flaw in a module is reachable when its route is exposed and accepts attacker-controlled input. That is a property of how Drupal modules work, not a finding about this CVE.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Impact
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The outcome list describes possible effects across the batch, not a guaranteed profile for every affected site. Remote code execution against a module compromises the hosting account. Privilege escalation widens a limited account. Data outcomes touch integrity and confidentiality. Cross-site scripting reaches authenticated sessions, administrative ones included.&lt;br&gt;
The German risk label "hoch" is the advisory's summary judgement for the group. It is not a per-identifier severity, and no per-identifier CVSS vector is published here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Affected products and scope
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sixteen projects, 19 fixed releases. Webform 6.2.12 and 6.3.1. Project Browser 2.0.3 and 2.1.5. Editoria11y Accessibility Checker 2.2.23 and 3.0.9. Webform REST 4.2.1. Cloud 7.0.1. Commerce Decoupled Checkout 1.8.0. Mermaid Diagram Field 1.0.9. CookieCuttr 2.0.3. REST &amp;amp; JSON API Authentication 3.2.0. Stop administrator login 1.6. Tawk.to Live chat application 3.0.4. AI CKEditor 1.4.3. Combined image style 1.0.7. CSS Usage Analyzer 1.0.2. Smart Content 3.2.1. Diba carousel slider 3.0.2.&lt;br&gt;
Drupal core is explicitly outside the advisory, which is worth stating because the project name alone often triggers that assumption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Exposure context
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 28 September 2026, ZoomEye returned 436,345 assets for app="Drupal" and zero for vul.cve="CVE-2026-96369". The fleet count is an index observation about Drupal deployments generally. The zero says the index has not linked assets to this identifier. Neither establishes that any particular site runs a vulnerable module, and the zero is not a clean bill of health.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Remediation and mitigations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the mechanism is unpublished, remediation has to proceed from version state rather than from a signature. Inventory the contributed projects you run, compare against the fixed releases above, and update what is affected on your branch.&lt;br&gt;
Then watch for the project-level advisories. Those are the documents that will name routes and parameters, and they are what a detection rule can actually be built from. Until they appear, request-log monitoring against affected module paths and file-integrity checks on module directories are the pragmatic interim measures.&lt;br&gt;
Write down what you concluded and what you assumed. When the project advisories land, revisiting a documented assumption takes minutes; reconstructing it takes hours.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CERT-BUND advisory WID-SEC-2026-3554, published 23 September 2026, high risk&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CERT-BUND structured advisory record, affected and fixed versions, CVSS v3.1 base 98, temporal 85&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ZoomEye search app="Drupal", executed 28 September 2026, exact count 436345&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google’s September 2026 spam update rolled out globally from 24 September and took &lt;strong&gt;13 days and 16 hours&lt;/strong&gt; to complete. &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/google-september-2026-spam-update-2/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google’s September 2026 spam update&lt;/a&gt; described it as a normal spam update, but its duration made it notably slower than the company’s previous spam update in August. For site owners, the important lesson is not to guess at a hidden cause. It is to treat rankings during a long rollout as incomplete data, monitor changes carefully and avoid making rushed website changes before the update has settled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The timing comes from the &lt;a href="https://status.search.google.com/summary?e=2" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google Search Status Dashboard entry for the September 2026 spam update&lt;/a&gt;. Google also said the update applied across all languages and locations, with a rollout that could take up to two weeks. That global scope means businesses with international audiences could see volatility in more than one market while the same update was still progressing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the longer rollout confirms, and what it does not
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google confirmed the rollout’s dates, worldwide scope and classification as a spam update. It did &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; publicly explain why this rollout lasted almost two weeks, nor did it disclose the specific signals or weighting behind ranking changes. A longer timeline should therefore not be treated as proof of an unusually severe penalty, a new ranking system or a problem with a particular website.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Spam updates are distinct from &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/proprietary-assets-google-march-2026-core-update/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;broad core updates&lt;/a&gt;. Based on Google’s description, this change focused on enforcing spam-related policies rather than conducting a general reassessment of content quality across every topic. A ranking movement during the period may be connected to the update, but Google’s public materials do not allow site owners to identify a precise trigger from timing alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The available rollout data does establish a meaningful difference from the prior spam update in 2026:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Update&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Reported rollout duration&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What the research confirms&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;August 2026 spam update&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Roughly 2 days and 16 hours&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A shorter 2026 spam update rollout&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;September 2026 spam update&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;13 days and 16 hours&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A global normal spam update and the longest spam rollout in 2026 to date&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The comparison is useful operationally. A short rollout may allow a clearer before-and-after assessment within days. A nearly two-week rollout creates a longer period in which rankings can move as Google’s changes reach different systems, languages and locations. It also leaves more room for ordinary factors, such as changing demand or competitor activity, to overlap with update-related volatility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Monitor performance without chasing daily fluctuations
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For businesses that depend on organic traffic, a long Google rollout is a reason to strengthen measurement, not to rewrite pages in response to every position change. The most useful evidence should come from first-party performance data and a consistent baseline from before the rollout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During the rollout window, site owners should:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review Search Console performance reports for changes in clicks, impressions, average position and affected queries.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/local-eeat-audit-ai-search-visibility/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Compare ranking history by country and language&lt;/a&gt; where the website serves multiple markets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identify whether declines are concentrated in a page type, query group or geographic segment rather than assuming a sitewide effect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Record the date and scope of material changes so post-rollout comparisons are based on evidence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Avoid major structural or content overhauls solely because of short-term movement during the update.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The final point matters because reacting midway through a rollout can make later analysis harder. If teams change templates, internal linking, content or technical settings while rankings are already moving, they may not be able to separate the effect of their own changes from Google’s update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How to assess the result after completion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once the update is complete, compare performance with a meaningful pre-update baseline rather than a single day of traffic. Look for sustained movement after the rollout, then examine the pages and queries involved. If a decline aligns with patterns that raise spam-policy concerns, the practical response is to review that area of the site and return to normal optimization work centered on high-quality, policy-compliant content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That does not mean every loss has a simple explanation. Google has not disclosed the September update’s detailed signals, so a ranking decline alone cannot establish which factor caused it. Conversely, a temporary dip that reverses after the rollout may not justify extensive intervention. The goal is a measured diagnosis based on stable trends, not a rapid attempt to reverse a movement that may still be in progress.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For companies investing in search as a dependable acquisition channel, visibility in &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/google-ai-overviews-branded-queries-demandsphere-data/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI-driven results&lt;/a&gt; and conventional search increasingly needs the same discipline: clear content, trustworthy site practices and evidence-led measurement. GEO Search Leads helps teams identify opportunities to improve &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://geosearchleads.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI search visibility and organic lead generation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, so they can focus resources on pages and topics that support qualified demand rather than react to noise. Start an AI visibility scan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;When did Google’s September 2026 spam update roll out?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google’s Search Status Dashboard shows that the rollout began on 24 September 2026 and lasted 13 days and 16 hours, implying completion around 8 October 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Was the September 2026 spam update global?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Google said the update applied globally, across all languages and locations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Was this a core update?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Google described it as a normal spam update, which is focused on spam-related policy enforcement rather than a broad core update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why did Google’s September spam update take longer than the August update?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google confirmed the rollout duration but did not publicly disclose why it took longer. The September rollout lasted 13 days and 16 hours, compared with roughly 2 days and 16 hours for the August 2026 spam update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What should site owners do during a Google spam update?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Monitor Search Console and ranking history, including international segments where relevant. Avoid major changes based solely on short-term volatility, then assess sustained performance after the rollout is complete.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google’s September 2026 spam update was a confirmed global rollout with an unusually extended timeline for that year. Its duration does not reveal the underlying signals Google used, but it does reinforce a practical principle for site owners: measure changes across the full rollout, wait for stable patterns and base any response on evidence rather than daily ranking movement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cloud optimization after a lift-and-shift should start with measurement, not with bigger instance sizes. Most migrated apps run slower because the architecture still assumes one data center, where the app and the database sat a few feet apart. The cloud quietly broke that assumption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are a CTO or technical founder, you probably know this moment. The migration went live on schedule and nothing crashed. Then support tickets started saying pages feel sluggish. Your team moved to bigger instances. It helped a little, and the bill went up a lot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is usually the network, not the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why does a rehosted app run slower than it did on-prem?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A rehosted app runs slower because it carries on-prem habits into an environment that works differently. Network hops cost more, disks behave differently, and nothing scales unless you tell it what to watch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four causes show up again and again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chatty database calls across zones.&lt;/strong&gt; On-prem, your app server and database sat on the same switch. A round trip took a fraction of a millisecond, so code that made 200 small queries per page load never hurt. In the cloud, the app might run in one availability zone and the primary database in another. Each round trip now takes around a millisecond or more, and 200 of them add up to a delay users can see. N+1 query patterns that stayed hidden for years suddenly show up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Missing caching.&lt;/strong&gt; Old servers often had plenty of local memory and a warm file system cache that nobody set up on purpose. A fresh cloud instance starts cold, may have less RAM, and gets replaced on every deploy. Whatever the app quietly relied on is gone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Wrong storage tiers.&lt;/strong&gt; Migration tools usually map disks to a default general purpose volume. If your database did heavy random reads on a local SSD array, a baseline volume with burst credits will feel fine in testing. Then it slows down once the credits run out under real load. Throughput limits on the instance itself can cap you too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;No autoscaling signals.&lt;/strong&gt; Autoscaling only works when it scales on the right metric. Many teams set it to CPU because that is the default. But if your app is waiting on the database or a queue, CPU stays low while users wait, and nothing scales.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What does getting this wrong cost you?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The usual reaction, buying bigger instances, treats the symptom and hides the cause. You pay more every month and the slow pages stay slow, because a bigger server does not shorten a network round trip.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The longer it runs, the more it costs you in other ways. Users who hit a slow checkout or a slow dashboard leave without telling you. Your team spends sprints firefighting instead of shipping. When leadership asks why the cloud move made things worse, nobody has data, only guesses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fixing the wrong layer first also means rework. Teams refactor code that was never the bottleneck, or move to a new database when the old one only needed a different volume type.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Do you actually need outside help?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Maybe not. If your app is a single service, your team already has tracing in place, and the slowdown is limited to one or two endpoints, you can likely fix it in-house by following the order below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Outside help makes sense when the slowdown is spread across the whole app, when nobody on the team has run this cloud provider in production before, or when you have already resized twice with no real improvement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should you measure first?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Measure where the time goes on a single slow request, end to end, before you change anything. One distributed trace of a slow page tells you more than a week of CPU graphs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask your team for these four numbers, in this order:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;p95 and p99 latency per endpoint&lt;/strong&gt;, not averages. Averages hide the slow tail that users actually complain about.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Database round trips per request&lt;/strong&gt;, and the time spent in them. If one page makes hundreds of queries, you have found your first fix.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zone placement&lt;/strong&gt; of app servers, the database primary, the cache and any internal services. Draw it on a whiteboard. Cross-zone hops on the hot path are a red flag.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Storage metrics&lt;/strong&gt;: IOPS, throughput, queue depth and burst balance on every volume attached to the database. A burst balance that trends toward zero explains slowdowns that only appear at busy hours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every major cloud has tools for this. AWS has CloudWatch and X-Ray, Azure has Application Insights, and Google Cloud has Cloud Trace. OpenTelemetry works across all of them if you want to stay portable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should you change first, and in what order?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Change the cheapest thing that removes the biggest delay, then measure again. Here is the order that works, with a decision rule for each step.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Fix placement.&lt;/strong&gt; If the app and the database primary sit in different zones, move the app instances that serve the hot path into the same zone as the primary, and keep a standby elsewhere for resilience. The trade-off: one zone outage now affects more of that tier, and in return you get shorter round trips. This is often a configuration change, not a code change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Cut round trips.&lt;/strong&gt; If a request makes more than a few dozen queries, batch them, use joins or eager-load related records. This is code work, but it is usually narrow. A handful of endpoints tend to cause most of the pain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Add caching where reads repeat.&lt;/strong&gt; If the same data is read many times and changes rarely, put it in a managed cache such as Redis or Memcached, and serve static assets through a CDN. Agree the expiry rules with the product owner, because how stale data can be is a business question, not only a technical one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Right-size storage.&lt;/strong&gt; If burst balance drops or queue depth climbs under load, move the database to a provisioned IOPS volume or a managed database service. Check that the instance type supports the throughput you are paying for, or the new volume gets capped anyway.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Scale on the right signal.&lt;/strong&gt; If the app waits on I/O, scale on requests per target, queue length or latency instead of CPU. Set a cooldown so it does not flap, and load test the rule before you trust it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;6. Only then, resize compute.&lt;/strong&gt; Once the first five steps are done, the data usually shows what instance size you actually need. Often it is smaller than what you run today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should you ask your team or vendor?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask questions that force evidence, not opinions. These work well:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can you show me one trace of our slowest page, with the time spent at each hop?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which zone is each component in, and why?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How many database queries does our busiest endpoint make per request?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What is the burst balance on the database volume at peak hours?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What metric does autoscaling use, and when did it last trigger?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What will you change first, and how will we know it worked?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the answer to the last question is a bigger instance, push back and ask for the trace first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Cloud optimization after a lift-and-shift is a phase, not a failure
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lift-and-shift is a valid first step. It gets you out of the data center on a timeline you can control. Running slower afterwards does not mean the migration failed. It means the tuning work has started, and that work goes fastest when you measure first and change one layer at a time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Geminate Solutions works on production systems at real scale, including 10M+ requests per minute handled in production (exam platform). If you want a second opinion on your migrated setup, our &lt;a href="https://geminatesolutions.com/services/cloud-devops?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=2026-10-15-d2" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cloud and DevOps team&lt;/a&gt; will do a free review of your architecture and reply within 24 hours.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Joule Work is the AI interface SAP added to its business software, a layer where an employee asks a question or hands a task to an agent and the work happens inside the SAP applications that hold the data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SAP began the customer rollout of Joule Work at SAP Connect on 6 October 2026, and general availability for the new assistants and agents starts in October.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Joule Work is already live with 110,000 SAP employees, and SAP reports 20% productivity gains across finance, HR and procurement from that internal deployment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The interface rests on three pillars SAP describes as conversations, spaces and Joule Studio, with customers controlling the level of autonomy given to what the assistants execute.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The assistant is grounded in SAP Knowledge Graph, which SAP says maps more than 7 million data fields, and Joule can reach third-party agents through the Agent2Agent protocol.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Named customers put statements on the record at the event: Novartis described a Sourcing Assistant pilot, Morgan Foods described agentic adoption on its own technical foundation, Nestlé described collections ranked by risk, and PwC described earlier detection of billing issues.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SAP also entered payments with SAP Pay, a service that executes and reconciles payment inside SAP Cloud ERP once an invoice is approved, generally available in the United States and the United Kingdom.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A buyer evaluating this stack can ask for the same four things an internal rollout produces: a named owner for the data grounding, the audit trail for each executed action, the record of who approved the autonomy level, and the measurement plan that produced the productivity figure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What SAP put on general release in October
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SAP's press release from SAP Connect describes a rollout with dates attached. "Joule Work is beginning its customer rollout as a new AI user interface that lets employees interact with SAP solutions in their own language and complete work across SAP and third-party data," the release states, and the accompanying Innovation News Guide adds that general availability for these innovations starts in October.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fi6q79o46mdi8uancrt76.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fi6q79o46mdi8uancrt76.png" alt="Capture of the SAP newsroom press release page for SAP Puts the Autonomous Enterprise to Work, published after SAP Connect 2026." width="800" height="700"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Capture of SAP's own press release page, "SAP Puts the Autonomous Enterprise to Work" (SAP News, news.sap.com, published 6 October 2026, retrieved 9 October 2026): the vendor's publication of the rollout timeline, the internal productivity figures and the customer statements cited here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reporting line inside SAP carries the interesting evidence. "At SAP, Joule Work is already live with 110,000 employees, with the company seeing 20% productivity gains across finance, HR and procurement," the press release states. Internal deployment at that scale gives a vendor something a customer reference cannot: a population large enough that the average is hard to dress up, measured on the vendor's own processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Christian Klein, SAP's chief executive, framed the customer promise at the keynote in the same terms, saying that from the rollout the more than 400 million SAP users can talk in their own language to SAP and Joule Work gets the job done. A promise of that size is the reason the released figures matter more than the released features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The three pillars of Joule Work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SAP describes the product as three connected pieces, and the split is worth reading closely because it maps onto how a business group would adopt it. Conversations are where users ask questions and delegate tasks to agents. Spaces display content, insights and shared work. Joule Studio is where customers extend Joule or build agents for their own processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That third piece is the one that decides whether the rollout stays a licence and turns into a system. A team that can assemble an agent inside Joule Studio from its own process knowledge keeps the definition of the work, and the definition of the work is where the quality of the output is decided.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SAP's own framing of the assistant's reach goes one step further through the Agent2Agent protocol, which the press release says lets Joule connect with third-party AI and agents so that governed business context travels into broader AI workflows. Interoperability at that layer matters for a company that runs one vendor's ERP and another vendor's models, which describes most of the mid-size market we work with at &lt;a href="https://neticslabs.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Netics&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How SAP grounded the assistant in its own data
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The grounding claim is the technical claim in this release. "Grounded in business context from SAP Knowledge Graph, which maps more than 7 million data fields, Joule provides information users can rely on," the press release states, and the keynote write-up expands the same graph to 50 million tables, more than 50,000 public and private APIs and 400 data products.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fc6ld6xv05g85g348or3y.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fc6ld6xv05g85g348or3y.webp" alt="Netics editorial metric grid with the Joule Work figures SAP published: more than 7 million data fields, 110,000 internal users, 20% productivity gains and 50% less trade classification effort." width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Netics editorial card built from SAP's SAP Connect 2026 press release and Innovation News Guide: the figures SAP published for Joule Work and the internal deployment behind them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two things follow from a knowledge graph that large. The first is that an answer can name the field it came from, which is what separates a grounded response from a fluent one. The second is the work it implies on the customer side: a graph of SAP's own data fields does not describe a company's warehouse locations, approval limits or payment terms until somebody maps them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That mapping is the same discipline we build into &lt;a href="https://neticslabs.com/en/solutions/pipelines" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;automation pipelines that keep a human approval step&lt;/a&gt;, because an agent executing work inside an ERP touches money and obligations. SAP's own product split acknowledges the stakes when it says Joule Assistants "execute operational work directly within business workflows, using established data, authorizations and audit trails," with customers controlling the level of autonomy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the named customers describe
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Six customers spoke at the event, and their statements describe how far each company has got with adoption, which makes them useful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Novartis is in pilot: "We've co-developed and recently launched a pilot of SAP's Joule Sourcing Assistant," said Christoph Buerki, the company's head of procurement. Morgan Foods is building foundations, with its chief financial officer Steve Hankins saying the company is putting the pieces in place for agentic adoption through its technical foundation, and adding that its superpower will come from people on top of agents. Nestlé described an S/4HANA transformation across 140 countries that lets Joule and the Accounts Receivable Assistant prioritise collections work by risk. PwC described a Billing Assistant that identifies potential issues earlier in a process it calls error-prone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read together, the statements trace a maturity line from pilot to production process, and none of them quotes a cost saving for a customer. The productivity number in this release belongs to SAP itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


SAP Pay moves payments inside the ERP workflow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxzgstl5au84zqdfeldum.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxzgstl5au84zqdfeldum.webp" alt="Netics editorial scorecard listing what each named SAP Connect customer described, from the Novartis sourcing pilot to the PwC billing assistant." width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Netics editorial card built from SAP's SAP Connect 2026 press release: the adoption stage each named customer described, in the words the company published.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The payments announcement sits further along the same reasoning. SAP Pay is a payment service embedded in SAP Cloud ERP workflows, powered by Tereina, a regulated payment-service provider SAP founded in April 2026. "When an invoice is approved in an SAP Cloud ERP application, SAP Pay automatically executes payment and reconciles it against the corresponding invoice or purchase order," the Innovation News Guide states, and the same page records that SAP Pay is generally available now in the United States and United Kingdom.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Payments are the place where an automated step has the least room for a wrong decision, which is why the design detail that matters here is the trigger: an approval that has already happened inside the ERP. Reconciliation then happens against the document that was approved, so the audit trail and the money movement share one system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Elsewhere in the suite the release offers one customer-side number: early deployments of the International Trade Assistant reduced trade classification effort by up to 50%. That figure arrives with the qualifier "up to", which is the honest way to publish a range that depends on the tariff schedule in front of it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What a buyer can check before signing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rollout raises the same four questions our own clients ask about agentic software, and SAP's publication answers some of them better than a brochure would.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask for the record behind the productivity figure: which processes were measured, over what period, and whether the comparison baseline was a human-only workflow. Ask how the knowledge graph gets your data, and who owns the mapping when a warehouse moves or a payment term changes. Ask what the audit trail contains for an action the assistant executed, since an approver's name and a timestamp are the artifacts an auditor will request. Ask for the autonomy setting in writing, including who can raise it and what review period applies afterwards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A vendor that has run this internally for 110,000 people has the material to answer all four. The published evidence so far covers the first question and describes the other three as product capabilities, which is normal at this stage of a rollout and worth tracking as customer deployments start producing their own numbers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F2wln0spejydgyn1f5viw.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F2wln0spejydgyn1f5viw.png" alt="Capture of the SAP newsroom keynote article page describing the Joule Work pillars and the SAP Connect 2026 keynote." width="800" height="700"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Capture of SAP's own keynote write-up, "SAP Showcases the Autonomous Enterprise in Action" (SAP News, news.sap.com, published 6 October 2026, retrieved 9 October 2026): the vendor's account of the three Joule Work pillars, the knowledge graph dimensions and the customer rollout statement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An ERP assistant arrives with the data model, the authorisations and the audit trail already in place, and that inheritance is the reason this rollout is worth watching. The vendor did the part that is hard to fake, which is running it on itself first, and the part that remains for every buyer is the mapping of their own processes and the measurement of their own before-and-after.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://blog.neticslabs.com/sap-joule-work-autonomous-enterprise-customer-rollout/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the Netics blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your coding agent can change a headline in seconds. Finding out whether that headline helps people sign up takes an experiment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built &lt;a href="https://github.com/splithub-io/splithub-sdk" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SplitHub&lt;/a&gt; to keep that experiment in the same workflow as the code. You declare variants and a goal in &lt;code&gt;splithub.config.ts&lt;/code&gt;, render the assigned value in your app, and read results from the CLI or an MCP client.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Start in your app
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SplitHub needs Node.js 22.13 or newer. Run:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx splithub init
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For your own database, use:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx splithub init &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--mode&lt;/span&gt; self
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The React SDK provides &lt;code&gt;SplitHubProvider&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useVariant&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useValue&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useTrack&lt;/code&gt;. The Next.js package adds middleware and server component helpers. Assignment uses a local hash, so choosing a variant doesn't require a network request during rendering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Give your agent the tools
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To connect the MCP server in Claude Code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;claude mcp add splithub &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--&lt;/span&gt; npx &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-y&lt;/span&gt; @splithub/mcp
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For another MCP client, the configuration is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"mcpServers"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"splithub"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"command"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"npx"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"args"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"-y"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@splithub/mcp"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You can then ask it to create a headline test, start or pause an experiment, push config, or read results. The server uses the same project config and credentials as the CLI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Test a shorter headline on the pricing page, using signup as the goal.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Later:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Read the results for the pricing headline test. Is there enough evidence to choose a variant?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CLI also lets you inspect results directly:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx splithub status
npx splithub results hero-title
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Replace &lt;code&gt;hero-title&lt;/code&gt; with your experiment key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Cloud or your own storage
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SplitHub Cloud returns statistical verdicts. Self-hosted storage returns raw visitors, conversions, conversion rates and revenue. The public SDK is MIT licensed; the cloud analysis is separate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You still need real visitors and a measured goal. An agent can help operate a test, but it can't replace evidence with a confident answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The repository includes complete &lt;a href="https://github.com/splithub-io/splithub-sdk/tree/main/examples" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Vite + React and Next.js examples&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/splithub-io/splithub-sdk" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Explore the SDK on GitHub&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href="https://splithub.io/docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Questions or feedback: &lt;a href="mailto:info@splithub.io"&gt;info@splithub.io&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React provides several Hooks to manage state in functional components. One of the important Hooks used for managing complex state logic is the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; Hook&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For simple state updates, the &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; Hook is usually sufficient. However, when an application contains multiple related state updates or complex conditions, &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; can help organize the logic in a more structured and maintainable way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this blog, we will explore what &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; is, how it works, its syntax and real-world applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Is the useReducer Hook?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; Hook is a built-in React Hook that manages component state using a reducer function. It allows developers to define how the state should change based on different actions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of writing state-update logic in multiple event handlers, developers can centralize the logic inside a reducer function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; Hook is particularly useful when several actions can modify the same state or when state updates involve multiple conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Syntax of useReducer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The basic syntax of &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useReducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;initialState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The syntax contains four important concepts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State:&lt;/strong&gt; Represents the current state value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Dispatch:&lt;/strong&gt; A function used to send an action to React.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reducer:&lt;/strong&gt; A function that determines the next state based on the current state and the action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Initial State:&lt;/strong&gt; The starting value of the state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these concepts is essential to using &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; effectively.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How Does useReducer Work?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; Hook follows a structured process whenever a user performs an action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The user interacts with the application.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The component dispatches an action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React processes the action using the reducer function.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The reducer calculates and returns the next state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React renders the component using the updated state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The process can be summarized as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;User Action → Dispatch → Reducer → New State → UI Update&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, in a counter application, clicking the Increment button dispatches an increment action. The reducer receives the current count and the action, calculates the next count, and returns it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the current count is &lt;code&gt;5&lt;/code&gt;, the reducer returns &lt;code&gt;6&lt;/code&gt;. React then updates the displayed count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Understanding the Reducer Function
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reducer function is the core part of &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;. It accepts two parameters: the current state and an action.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;reducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;switch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;decrement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nl"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In this example, the reducer handles two actions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When the action type is &lt;code&gt;"increment"&lt;/code&gt;, the reducer increases the state by one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When the action type is &lt;code&gt;"decrement"&lt;/code&gt;, the reducer decreases the state by one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;code&gt;default&lt;/code&gt; case returns the current state if the action type is not recognized.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reducer should be a &lt;strong&gt;pure function&lt;/strong&gt;. It should calculate the next state from its inputs without modifying the existing state or performing side effects such as API requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Real-World Applications of useReducer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; Hook is useful in applications where state changes depend on different user actions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Shopping Cart Management
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An e-commerce application may need to add products, remove products, increase quantities, decrease quantities, and clear the cart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These operations can be represented as different actions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ADD_TO_CART&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, the action contains a type and a product object.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reducer can check whether the product already exists in the cart. If it does, the reducer can increase its quantity. Otherwise, it can add the product to the cart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; helps organize these related operations in one place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Form Management
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A registration form may contain a username, email address, password, and submission status.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reducer can handle actions such as updating individual fields, clearing the form, and recording submission results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;UPDATE_EMAIL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;user@example.com&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This approach can make complex form-state transitions easier to organize.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Game State Management
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A game might track the player's score, remaining lives, and whether the game has ended.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Actions such as &lt;code&gt;"INCREASE_SCORE"&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;"LOSE_LIFE"&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;"RESTART_GAME"&lt;/code&gt; can be handled by a reducer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This keeps the rules for updating the game state together, making them easier to understand and maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; Hook is an important part of React state management. It allows developers to manage state through actions and a reducer function, providing a structured approach to handling complex state transitions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By understanding state, actions, dispatch, and reducers, developers can build applications with more organized state-update logic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; remains an excellent choice for simple state management, &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt; can be particularly useful for applications such as shopping carts, complex forms, and games.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’ve launched 2059 Studio, a free collection of six tools built with React and TypeScript:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;• QR generation&lt;br&gt;
• Image resizing, compression, and conversion&lt;br&gt;
• Color palettes and contrast checks&lt;br&gt;
• Favicon and app icon generation&lt;br&gt;
• Social preview cards&lt;br&gt;
• JSON formatting and validation&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The architecture keeps user files and content in the browser. The separate Express/SQLite backend records aggregate tool opens and exports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One interesting QR issue was Unicode handling. The encoder’s default byte conversion caused Czech text to fail an exact-content decoding check. Supplying UTF-8 bytes explicitly fixed it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;QR exports are checked by decoding the rendered image and comparing the result with the original payload. This catches unreadable outputs, although physical print testing is still important.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Other details include lazy-loaded tool modules, CSV batch cancellation, locally saved QR styling presets, and embedded fonts for Czech PDF captions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Live site:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://tools.jiricech2059.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tools.jiricech2059.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’d welcome specific feedback on the QR workflow, mobile controls, and export experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Development and this write-up used AI assistance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Una pantalla de verificación de email puede parecer sencilla: el usuario pulsa un botón, espera y ve un mensaje. El problema aparece cuando la interfaz mezcla “solicitud enviada”, “mensaje recibido” y “cuenta verificada”. Son hechos distintos, pero muchas UI los presentan como si fueran uno solo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En proyectos frontend me resulta más fiable tratar la verificación como una pequeña máquina de estados. React se ocupa de renderizar cada estado y la capa de accesibilidad se asegura de que el cambio sea entendible para quien navega con teclado o lector de pantalla. El resultado suele ser menos brillante a primera vista, pero mucho mas honesto y fácil de probar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


El problema: enviado no significa verificado
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Después de pulsar “Enviar código”, la aplicación normalmente solo sabe que aceptó una petición. Todavía no sabe si el email llegó, si el código pertenece a esta sesión o si ya fue utilizado. Mostrar “verificado” en ese momento crea una promesa que la aplicación no puede sostener.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Una consulta de prueba como &lt;code&gt;temp org mail&lt;/code&gt; tampoco debería cambiar el estado de la interfaz. Es texto de búsqueda, no una señal de entrega ni una dirección válida. La UI debe reaccionar a eventos confiables del flujo, no a palabras parecidas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conviene separar, como mínimo, estos momentos:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;idle&lt;/code&gt;: aún no se ha pedido un código.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;sending&lt;/code&gt;: la petición está en curso.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;sent&lt;/code&gt;: el servidor aceptó la petición, pero falta el mensaje.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;checking&lt;/code&gt;: se está comprobando el código introducido.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;verified&lt;/code&gt;: la cuenta quedó verificada.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt;: algo falló y se puede explicar qué hacer después.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La diferencia entre &lt;code&gt;sent&lt;/code&gt; y &lt;code&gt;verified&lt;/code&gt; es pequeña en el código, pero enorme en la experiencia. También evita que un re-render o un doble clic convierta una respuesta antigua en un falso éxito.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Modela estados que el usuario pueda entender
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No hace falta una librería de máquina de estados para empezar. Un estado discriminado hace explícitas las transiciones y permite que el diseño acompañe a la lógica:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;sending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;sent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;checking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;verified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;El componente puede mostrar una instrucción diferente para cada &lt;code&gt;kind&lt;/code&gt;. En &lt;code&gt;sent&lt;/code&gt;, por ejemplo, debe pedir que se revise la bandeja y ofrecer “Reenviar” solo cuando corresponda. En &lt;code&gt;verified&lt;/code&gt;, debe mostrar una confirmación final, no dejar un spinner activo por si acaso.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Para un entorno de pruebas, un buzón temporal como &lt;a href="https://tempmailso.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;temp mail so&lt;/a&gt; puede ayudar a reproducir el flujo, pero el frontend sigue necesitando una señal clara del backend. La existencia de un buzón no equivale a que el código correcto haya sido consumido.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


React y aria-live sin robar el foco
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Los cambios de estado deben anunciarse sin mover el foco de forma agresiva. Un patrón útil es mantener el foco en el campo o botón que la persona está usando y añadir una región de estado:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;StatusMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;sending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Enviando el código…&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;sent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Código enviado. Revisa tu bandeja de entrada.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;checking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Comprobando el código…&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;verified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Email verificado correctamente.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-live&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"polite"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;role="status"&lt;/code&gt; suele ser suficiente para un cambio no urgente. Un error que impide continuar puede necesitar &lt;code&gt;role="alert"&lt;/code&gt;, pero usar alertas para cada actualización resulta demasido ruidoso. El texto debe ser corto, accionable y no desaparecer mientras alguien intenta leerlo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Si una transición lleva a un formulario distinto, entonces sí puede tener sentido mover el foco al nuevo encabezado o al primer campo. Hazlo una sola vez, después de que el contenido exista, y no en cada render. De lo contrario el usuario esperan poder escribir y el foco vuelve al principio sin aviso.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Diseña una espera ligera y medible
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un spinner infinito es una mala explicación. Mientras el estado es &lt;code&gt;sending&lt;/code&gt; o &lt;code&gt;checking&lt;/code&gt;, desactiva solo la acción que no debe repetirse y conserva el resto del contexto visible. El botón puede cambiar a “Enviando…” y volver a “Reenviar código” cuando termine la respuesta.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;También ayuda mostrar límites reales: “Puedes solicitar otro código cuando termine esta espera”. Si el servidor devuelve un identificador de intento o una fecha de expiración, guárdalos asociados a la sesión actual. No aceptes una respuesta de una solicitud anterior solo porque llegó mas tarde.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En el layout, reserva espacio para el mensaje de estado. Así un error no empuja el formulario de forma inesperada y se reduce el riesgo de cambios de diseño acumulados. Es el mismo principio que se aplica al &lt;a href="https://dev.to/silviutech/css-para-errores-de-email-sin-cls-4h2e"&gt;CSS para errores de email sin CLS&lt;/a&gt;: el mensaje puede aparecer sin hacer saltar la pantalla.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La métrica útil no es únicamente cuánto tarda la petición. Observa cuántos reenvíos ocurren, cuántos códigos caducan y cuántas personas corrigen un error sin abandonar. Estos datos conectan el pulido visual con una experiencia de usuario que realmente funciona.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pruebas de accesibilidad y estados de error
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prueba cada estado sin depender de que el flujo feliz termine bien. Con teclado, comprueba que el foco tenga un indicador visible y que se pueda llegar al botón de reenvío. Con un lector de pantalla, verifica que el cambio de &lt;code&gt;sent&lt;/code&gt; a &lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt; se anuncie una vez y con instrucciones concretas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En tests de React, renderiza el componente con cada &lt;code&gt;kind&lt;/code&gt; y comprueba el texto que ve la persona. Añade un test para una respuesta tardía: una verificación antigua no debe reemplazar un estado nuevo. También vale la pena probar el doble clic y la pérdida temporal de conexión.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Las &lt;a href="https://dev.to/alexcarteruk/kubernetes-correos-de-prueba-sin-falsos-positivos-1b8a"&gt;pruebas de correo sin falsos positivos&lt;/a&gt; complementan estas pruebas de UI. Una cubre la señal de entrega; la otra, la forma en que esa señal se comunica. Juntas dan más confianza que validar solo el color verde de un mensaje.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Preguntas frecuentes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


¿Debo mover el foco cuando se envía el código?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Normalmente no. Conserva el foco donde estaba y anuncia el cambio con una región de estado. Muévelo solo si aparece un contexto nuevo que requiere interacción inmediata.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


¿Puedo mostrar “verificado” cuando el servidor responde 200?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Solo si ese &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; representa la verificación completada. Si confirma únicamente que el email fue enviado, usa &lt;code&gt;sent&lt;/code&gt; y explica el siguiente paso.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


¿Qué hago con un código expirado?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Muestra un error específico, conserva el email visible y ofrece solicitar un código nuevo. Evita borrar todo el formulario: recuperar el contexto es mas amable y reduce trabajo repetido.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Una interfaz de verificación confiable no necesita ocultar la espera. Necesita nombrar cada estado, anunciar los cambios con cuidado y tratar los errores como parte normal del diseño. React hace fácil renderizar la pantalla; la calidad aparece cuando cada mensaje dice exactamente lo que la aplicación sabe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Beauty AR demo usually proves one thing: an effect can run on the developer’s device.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A production join flow has a harder contract. The user still needs to enter the call if effect initialization times out, the GPU context is lost, a low-end device cannot sustain processing, or an asset fails to load.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction helps clarify the role of “AI in the browser.” Real-time computer vision can produce useful beauty filters, makeup, segmentation, avatars, and gesture-driven effects. It cannot decide how much delay is acceptable, whether an altered image may be published without confirmation, or what the application should do when processing fails. Those remain product and engineering decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial, we will build a preflight gate with one invariant:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Beauty AR is a candidate output until it has produced frames and the user explicitly chooses to publish it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the candidate fails, the clean camera remains available. The call does not become dependent on the effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The workflow we want
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The browser join sequence will be:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Acquire the camera.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Show the clean preview.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prepare a Beauty AR candidate off-air.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verify that the candidate is producing frames before a deadline.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Let the user choose &lt;strong&gt;Join with effect&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;Join without effect&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Publish only the selected stream.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If the live effect later becomes unhealthy, attempt to replace it with the clean stream.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is deliberately different from testing whether an API call resolved. A resolved initialization promise does not prove that useful video is reaching the preview.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tencent RTC’s &lt;a href="https://trtc.io/document/beauty-ar-overview" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Beauty AR overview&lt;/a&gt; describes real-time capabilities including beauty filters, makeup, stickers, virtual backgrounds, avatars, gesture recognition, and image or video enhancement. Its &lt;a href="https://trtc.io/document/66968" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;low-end device optimization guide&lt;/a&gt; recommends adapting effects to device tiers, using appropriate performance modes, avoiding expensive segmentation or 3D/GAN effects where necessary, and controlling resolution and frame rate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our controller turns those recommendations into an application-level join policy without inventing a universal definition of a “fast device.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Model the states users can actually encounter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A boolean such as &lt;code&gt;beautyEnabled&lt;/code&gt; cannot distinguish loading from failure, qualification, publication, or recovery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use explicit phases instead:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Phase&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;acquiring-camera&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;camera-ready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;preparing-effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;qualifying-effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ready-clean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ready-effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;publishing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;live-clean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;live-effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;blocked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ViewState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;notice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;These states produce a straightforward UI contract:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Phase&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;User-visible behavior&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;acquiring-camera&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Disable join and show camera setup progress&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;camera-ready&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Show the clean preview&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;preparing-effect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keep the clean preview visible; allow cancellation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;qualifying-effect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Indicate that the effect is being checked&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;ready-clean&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Enable clean join and explain why the effect is unavailable, if relevant&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;ready-effect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Show both join choices; do not select the effect silently&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;publishing&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prevent duplicate join actions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;live-clean&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;live-effect&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Display which output is currently being sent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;blocked&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explain the camera or publication failure and offer a relevant retry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important emotional detail is that “your device could not run this effect” should not be presented as “your device cannot join.” Those are separate capabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Create the reproducible project
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with a framework-independent TypeScript core. It can later sit behind React, Vue, or another UI.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;mkdir &lt;/span&gt;beauty-preflight
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd &lt;/span&gt;beauty-preflight
npm init &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-y&lt;/span&gt;
npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-D&lt;/span&gt; typescript vitest @types/node
npx tsc &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--init&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Add these scripts to &lt;code&gt;package.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"scripts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"test"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"vitest run"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"test:watch"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"vitest"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For deterministic tests, the core uses a small &lt;code&gt;Stream&lt;/code&gt; value rather than requiring real browser media objects:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/preflight.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;EffectProfile&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;allowSegmentation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;allow3D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;allowGAN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CameraPort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;():&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;BeautyPort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;EffectProfile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispose&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;FrameProbe&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;hasProgress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;windowMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PublisherPort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;publish&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;unpublish&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;():&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Phase&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;acquiring-camera&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;camera-ready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;preparing-effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;qualifying-effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ready-clean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ready-effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;publishing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;live-clean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;live-effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;blocked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ViewState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;notice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Policy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;prepareDeadlineMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;qualificationWindowMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;CameraPort&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;BeautyPort&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;PublisherPort&lt;/code&gt; are application-owned boundaries. They are not names of Tencent RTC APIs. The production adapters should call the documented browser, Beauty AR, and RTC integration surfaces available to your application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Race the preparation, but clean up the loser
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A timeout does not cancel the operation underneath it. If initialization finishes after the application has fallen back, that late result must be disposed rather than attached to the preview.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/preflight.ts&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DeadlineResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;beforeDeadline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;deadlineMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DeadlineResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;ReturnType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DeadlineResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;deadlineMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;settled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DeadlineResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;race&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;settled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;clearTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The deadline is an application policy, not a Tencent RTC performance guarantee. Choose it from your join experience requirements and verify it on representative devices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Implement the preflight coordinator
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The coordinator retains the clean camera while the effect is prepared. An incrementing epoch invalidates callbacks from cancelled or superseded attempts.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/preflight.ts&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BeautyPreflight&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ViewState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;epoch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;candidate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;constructor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;camera&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CameraPort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;beauty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;BeautyPort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;probe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;FrameProbe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;publisher&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PublisherPort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;policy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Policy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ViewState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;snapshot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;():&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ViewState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;transition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ViewState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;onState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
